#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:1
msgid "Constant Interpreter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:7
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:9
msgid ""
"The bytecode interpreter aims to replace the existing AST traversal-based "
"evaluator in Clang, improving performance on constructs which are executed "
"inefficiently by the evaluator. The interpreter is activated by passing `-"
"fexperimental-new-constant-interpreter` to clang."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:14
msgid ""
"Since Clang 23, the bytecode interpreter can also be enabled by default by "
"passing `-DCLANG_USE_EXPERIMENTAL_CONST_INTERP=ON` to cmake. In that case, "
"it can be deactivated again via `-fno-experimental-new-constant-interpreter`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:19
msgid "Bytecode Compilation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:21
msgid ""
"Bytecode compilation is handled in `Compiler.h` for statements and for "
"expressions. The compiler has two different backends: one to generate "
"bytecode for functions (`ByteCodeEmitter`) and one to directly evaluate "
"expressions as they are compiled, without generating bytecode "
"(`EvalEmitter`). All functions are compiled to bytecode, while toplevel "
"expressions used in constant contexts are directly evaluated since the "
"bytecode would never be reused. This mechanism aims to pave the way towards "
"replacing the evaluator, improving its performance on functions and loops, "
"while being just as fast on single-use toplevel expressions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:32
msgid ""
"The interpreter relies on stack-based, strongly-typed opcodes. The glue "
"logic between the code generator, along with the enumeration and description "
"of opcodes, can be found in `Opcodes.td`. The opcodes are implemented as "
"generic template methods in `Interp.h` and instantiated with the relevant "
"primitive types by the interpreter loop or by the evaluating emitter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:39
msgid "Primitive Types"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:41
msgid "`PT_{U|S}int{8|16|32|64}`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:43
msgid ""
"Signed or unsigned integers of a specific bit width. 1-byte types are also 1 "
"byte in size. Sizes >= 16 bits are implemented using the `Integral` class. "
"They take up 24 bytes each since they need to be able to represent a pointer "
"that has been casted to an integer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:48
msgid "`PT_IntAP{S}`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:50
msgid ""
"Signed or unsigned integers of an arbitrary, but fixed width used to "
"implement integral types which are required by the target, but are not "
"supported by the host. Under the hood, they rely on `APInt`. The `Integral` "
"specialisation for these types is required by opcodes to share an "
"implementation with fixed integrals."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:56
msgid "`PT_Bool`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:58
msgid ""
"Representation for boolean types, essentially a 1-bit unsigned `Integral`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:61
msgid "`PT_Float`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:63
msgid ""
"Arbitrary, but fixed precision floating point numbers. Could be specialised "
"in the future similarly to integers in order to improve floating point "
"performance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:67
msgid "`PT_Ptr`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:69
msgid ""
"Pointer type, defined in `\"Pointer.h\"`. The most common type of pointer is "
"a \"BlockPointer\", which points to an `interp::Block`. But other pointer "
"types exist, such as typeid pointers or integral pointers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:74
msgid "`PT_MemberPtr`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:76
msgid ""
"Member pointer type, can also be a null member pointer. Defined in "
"`\"MemberPointer.h\"`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:79
msgid "Composite types"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:81
msgid ""
"The interpreter distinguishes two kinds of composite types: arrays and "
"records (structs and classes). Unions are represented as records, except at "
"most a single field can be marked as active. The contents of inactive fields "
"are kept until they are reactivated and overwritten. Complex numbers "
"(`_Complex`) and vectors (`__attribute((vector_size(16)))`) are treated as "
"arrays."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:88
msgid "Bytecode Execution"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:90
msgid ""
"Bytecode is executed using a stack-based interpreter. The execution context "
"consists of an `InterpStack`, along with a chain of `InterpFrame` objects "
"storing the call frames. Frames are built by call instructions and destroyed "
"by return instructions. They perform one allocation to reserve space for all "
"locals in a single block. These objects store all the required information "
"to emit stack traces whenever evaluation fails."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:98
msgid "Memory Organisation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:100
msgid ""
"Memory management in the interpreter relies on 3 data structures: `Block` "
"objects which store the data and associated inline metadata, `Pointer` "
"objects which refer to or into blocks, and `Descriptor` structures which "
"describe blocks and subobjects nested inside blocks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:105
msgid "Blocks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:107
msgid ""
"Blocks contain data interleaved with metadata. They are allocated either "
"statically in the code generator (globals, static members, dummy parameter "
"values etc.) or dynamically in the interpreter, when creating the frame "
"containing the local variables of a function. Blocks are associated with a "
"descriptor that characterises the entire allocation, along with a few "
"additional attributes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:114
msgid ""
"`IsStatic` indicates whether the block has static duration in the "
"interpreter, i.e. it is not a local in a frame."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:116
msgid ""
"`DeclID` identifies each global declaration (it is set to an invalid and "
"irrelevant value for locals) in order to prevent illegal writes and reads "
"involving globals and temporaries with static storage duration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:120
msgid ""
"Static blocks are never deallocated, but local ones might be deallocated "
"even when there are live pointers to them. Pointers are only valid as long "
"as the blocks they point to are valid, so a block with pointers to it whose "
"lifetime ends is kept alive until all pointers to it go out of scope. Since "
"the frame is destroyed on function exit, such blocks are turned into a "
"`DeadBlock` and copied to storage managed by the interpreter itself, not the "
"frame. Reads and writes to these blocks are illegal and cause an appropriate "
"diagnostic to be emitted. When the last pointer goes out of scope, dead "
"blocks are also deallocated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:130
msgid ""
"The lifetime of blocks is managed through 2 methods stored in the descriptor "
"of the block:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:133
msgid ""
"**CtorFn**: initializes the metadata which is stored in the block, alongside "
"actual data. Invokes the default constructors of objects which are not "
"trivial (`Pointer`, `Floating`, etc.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:136
msgid "**DtorFn**: invokes the destructors of non-trivial objects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:138
msgid ""
"Blocks track all the pointers into them through an intrusive doubly-linked "
"list, required to adjust and invalidate all pointers when transforming a "
"block into a dead block. If the lifetime of an object ends, all pointers to "
"it are invalidated, emitting the appropriate diagnostics when dereferenced."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:144
msgid ""
"Records are laid out identically to arrays of composites: each field and "
"base class is preceded by an inline descriptor. The `InlineDescriptor` saves "
"information about the initialization state, constness, mutability, lifetime, "
"etc. of a field."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:149
msgid "Consider this struct:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:158
msgid ""
"When allocating space for `s`, we allocate 24 bytes (not counting the "
"`Descriptor` instances we created for the `Record` and the field). The field "
"`c` needs 8 bytes, since we align to pointer size (this example uses a 64 "
"bit system). The `InlineDescriptor` preceding the field data uses up the "
"remaining 16 bytes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:163
msgid ""
"Inline descriptors are filled in by the `CtorFn` of blocks, which leaves "
"storage in an uninitialised, but valid state."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:166
msgid "Descriptors"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:168
msgid ""
"Descriptors are generated at bytecode compilation time and contain "
"information required to determine if a particular memory access is allowed "
"in constexpr. They also carry all the information required to emit a "
"diagnostic involving a memory access, such as the declaration which "
"originates the block. Currently there is a single kind of descriptor "
"encoding information for all block types."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:175
msgid "Pointers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:177
msgid "Pointers, implemented in `Pointer.h` are represented as a tagged union."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:179
msgid ""
"**BlockPointer**: used to reference memory allocated and managed by the "
"interpreter, being the only pointer kind which allows dereferencing in the "
"interpreter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:182
msgid ""
"**TypeIDPointer**: tracks information for the opaque type returned by "
"`typeid`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:184
msgid ""
"**IntegralPointer**: a pointer formed from an integer, think `(int*)123`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:185
msgid "**FunctionPointer**: a pointer to a function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:187
msgid ""
"Besides the previously mentioned union, a number of other pointer-like types "
"have their own type:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:190
msgid "**FunctionPointer** tracks functions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:191
msgid "**MemberPointer** tracks C++ object members"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:193
msgid "BlockPointer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:195
msgid ""
"Block pointers track a `Pointee`, the block to which they point, along with "
"a `Base` and an `Offset`. The base identifies the innermost field, while the "
"offset points to an array element relative to the base (including one-past-"
"end pointers). The offset identifies the array element or field which is "
"referenced, while the base points to the outer object or array which "
"contains the field. These two fields allow all pointers to be uniquely "
"identified, disambiguated and characterised."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:203
msgid "As an example, consider the following structure:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:220
msgid ""
"On the target, `&a` and `&a.b.x` are equal. So are `&a.c[0]` and `&a.c[0]."
"a`. In the interpreter, all these pointers must be distinguished since they "
"are all allowed to address a distinct range of memory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:225
msgid ""
"In the interpreter, the object would require 240 bytes of storage and would "
"have its fields interleaved with metadata. The pointers which can be derived "
"to the object are illustrated in the following diagram:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:240
msgid ""
"The `Base` offset of all pointers points to the start of a field or an array "
"and is preceded by an inline descriptor (unless `Base` is zero, pointing to "
"the root). All the relevant attributes can be read from either the inline "
"descriptor or the descriptor of the block."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:245
msgid ""
"Array elements are identified by the `Offset` field of pointers, pointing to "
"past the inline descriptors for composites and before the actual data in the "
"case of primitive arrays. The `Offset` points to the offset where primitives "
"can be read from. As an example, `a.c + 1` would have the same base as `a.c` "
"since it is an element of `a.c`, but its offset would point to `&a.c[1]`. "
"The array-to-pointer decay operation adjusts a pointer to an array (where "
"the offset is equal to the base) to a pointer to the first element."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:254
msgid "TypeInfoPointer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:256
msgid ""
"`TypeInfoPointer` tracks two types: the type assigned to `std::type_info` "
"and the type which was passed to `typeinfo`. It is part of the tagged union "
"in `Pointer`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:260
msgid "Interpretation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ConstantInterpreter.md:262
msgid ""
"After bytecode has been generated (or not, for expressions), the bytecode is "
"then interpreted. The bytecode is stack-based and uses `InterpStack` to "
"allocate memory for the produced values."
msgstr ""

